  "question": "If $y = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right)$ for $x \\in (-1,1)$, what is $d^2y/dx^2$ at $x=0$?",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "We have $y = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right)$<br /><br />But $\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2} = \\sin(2\\tan^{-1}x)$<br /><br />Thus $y = 2\\tan^{-1}x$<br /><br />Now $\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{1 + x^2}$<br /><br />$\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{2x}{(1 + x^2)^2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{4x}{(1 + x^2)^2}$<br /><br />At $x = 0$, $d^2y/dx^2 = 0$<br /><br />Answer: (A) 0",
